        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(process): resolve gemini .cmd shim on Windows in supervisor child adapter",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n- On Windows, using the `google-gemini-cli` provider fails with `spawn gemini ENOENT` because Node's `child_process.spawn` does not automatically resolve `.cmd` extensions (only `.exe`/`.com`)\n- npm global installs on Windows create `.cmd` shim files, so `gemini` (and `claude`) need the `.cmd` suffix to be found by `spawn`\n- Fixes #98573\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe supervisor child adapter (`src/process/supervisor/adapters/child.ts`) has a `resolveCommand` function that uses `resolveWindowsCommandShim` with a hardcoded allowlist of commands that should get `.cmd` appended on Windows. The list included `npm`, `pnpm`, `yarn`, and `npx` but not `gemini` or `claude` — so spawning those CLI tools failed on Windows.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- Windows users can now use the `google-gemini-cli` and `claude-cli` providers without encountering `ENOENT` spawn errors\n- No change on macOS or Linux (platform guard already skips non-Windows)\n\n## Evidence\n\n### Behavior or issue addressed\n- On Windows, `spawn(\"gemini\")` fails with ENOENT because Node only auto-resolves `.exe`/`.com` extensions, not `.cmd`\n- The `resolveCommand` helper was missing `gemini` from its shim allowlist\n\n### Real environment tested\n- Local development environment (Linux, Node 22)\n- Unit tests simulate Windows platform via `setPlatform(\"win32\")` to verify cmd.exe wrapping behavior\n\n### Exact steps or command run after this patch\n```bash\npnpm test src/process/supervisor/adapters/child.test.ts\n```\n\n### Evidence after fix\n```\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  21 passed (21)\n```\n\nNew regression tests added:\n- `wraps gemini CLI command through trusted cmd.exe on Windows (#98573)` — verifies `gemini` → `gemini.cmd` wrapped via `cmd.exe` on Windows\n- `wraps claude CLI command through trusted cmd.exe on Windows` — verifies `claude` → `claude.cmd` wrapped via `cmd.exe` on Windows\n- `keeps gemini command unchanged on non-Windows platforms (#98573)` — verifies no `.cmd` suffix on Linux/macOS\n\nExisting test `wraps Windows command shims through trusted cmd.exe` (for `pnpm`) already validates the same code path — the new entries follow the identical pattern.\n\n### Observed result after fix\n- On simulated Windows: `spawnArgs.argv` = `[cmd.exe, /d, /s, /c, \"gemini.cmd --version\"]` (shim resolved)\n- On Linux: `spawnArgs.argv` includes `gemini` verbatim (no `.cmd` suffix)\n\n### What was not tested\n- Not tested on a real Windows machine (no Windows environment available). The unit tests use `setPlatform(\"win32\")` to simulate the Windows code path and verify the exact spawn arguments.\n\n## Tests and validation\n\n```bash\npnpm test src/process/supervisor/adapters/child.test.ts  # 21 tests pass (was 18, +3 new)\n```\n\n## Risk checklist\n\n- [x] No: Does this change affect user-visible behavior? (Only affects Windows CLI tool spawning, restoring expected behavior)\n- [x] No: Does this change affect configuration surface?\n- [x] No: Does this change affect security? (No change to security model; same pattern used for other CLI tools like pnpm/npm/yarn)\n- [x] No: Does this change affect plugin APIs?\n- [x] No: Does this change require documentation updates?\n\n## Additional notes\n\nAI-assisted fix. Similar to how `npm`, `pnpm`, `yarn`, and `npx` were already handled, `gemini` and `claude` now also get the `.cmd` shim resolution on Windows. The `resolveWindowsCommandShim` function in `src/process/windows-command.ts` already handles the platform check and extension detection — the only missing piece was the allowlist entry.\n\nThe sibling PR #98609 targets the same issue with a similar approach. Compared to that PR, this one also covers `claude` (which has the same Windows spawn issue) and includes tests for the non-Windows path.",
        "branch": "fix/gemini-cli-windows-cmd-shim",
